# /etc/services - the programs init supervises.
#
# One per line. init starts each at boot and restarts it when it dies.
# Comments (#) and blank lines are ignored.
#
# How this differs from /etc/rc:
#   rc        runs once and finishes. Nothing revives what it started.
#   services  init keeps watching. If it dies, it comes back.
#
# The reason the SSH server belongs here is plain: if it died to a bug or
# to memory pressure, there would be no way left to reach the machine.
#
# Something that dies instantly gets a growing delay between restarts,
# and after 20 failures init gives up on it. That stops a busy loop.

# Log collector - FIRST, because everything below it has something to say.
#
#   Collects the kernel ring buffer and the /dev/log socket into
#   /data/log/messages, rotating at 512KB so it can never fill the card.
#   Without it, everything printed goes to the console and is gone as
#   soon as it scrolls. If the board wedges overnight there is no record
#   of what happened - which is when you most want one.
#
#   It used to be seventh in this file, after guard, wpa_supplicant,
#   dropbear, dhcp, ntp and cron. /dev/log does not exist until logd
#   creates it, so everything those six said while starting - which is
#   exactly when things go wrong - had nowhere to go. It has no
#   dependencies of its own, so there is no reason for it to wait.
logd

# The safety net. THIS LINE IS THE MOST IMPORTANT ONE IN THE FILE.
#
#   guard is what kills a runaway before it takes the board down, what
#   notices /data filling up, and what pushes a CPU hog to the back of
#   the queue so SSH still answers. Every one of those defences is this
#   one process.
#
#   It used to be started from /etc/rc, which runs once and finishes.
#   Nothing revived it. Killing guard therefore disabled the entire
#   self-defence of the machine permanently, and left no trace: the board
#   kept running, looked healthy, and was defenceless against the next
#   thing that went wrong. It was a worse fault than any of the things
#   guard protects against, because it was silent.
#
#   No -d here. init supervises a child that stays in the foreground;
#   a process that forks and lets the parent exit looks like an instant
#   death, and init would spend its life restarting it.
#
#   init never gives up on this one, however often it fails.
guard -r 32 -w 64

# WiFi: LP's own, `wpa` (userland/wpa). You talk to it with `lp-net`;
# `wifi` says at which step a connection stopped and why.
#
#   It replaced wpa_supplicant, under which the Pi associated and then
#   the WPA2 four-way handshake never happened, with every cause outside
#   wpa_supplicant ruled out. The same daemon runs the XPS's ath10k or
#   BCM4350 and the Pi's brcmfmac: the kernel's own CONNECT does the
#   association on both, and the handshake is ours.
#
#   No '?' condition any more. The old line asked for /sys/class/net/wlan0
#   because wpa_supplicant fails at once without it; `wpa` instead waits,
#   asleep, for any wireless interface to appear - the desktop's is not
#   called wlan0 (udev names it wlp2s0 or so), and a card whose firmware
#   loads late would otherwise have been given up on.
#
#   Saved networks: /etc/lp-net/networks on the desktop, /data/lp-net/
#   networks on a board - root-only, written atomically, rejoined at
#   boot. /etc/wpa.conf, which rc builds from the card's
#   wpa_supplicant.conf, is still read, so a Pi set up the old way keeps
#   connecting.
#
#   `touch /boot/wpa-debug` on the card, from any PC, still turns on the
#   frame-by-frame log (`wifi log`), because when the wireless will not
#   connect there is no other way to ask for it.
#
#   THE FALLBACK - wpa_supplicant, kept as the way back. It is switched
#   by a file, not by editing this line:
#       /boot/wpa-supplicant       on the card, from any PC
#       `sudo wifi fallback on`    (/etc/lp-net/fallback or
#                                   /data/lp-net/fallback)
#   With either present `wpa -d` becomes wpa_supplicant on the wireless
#   interface, with /etc/wpa.conf and the saved networks (the file is in
#   wpa_supplicant's format for this reason), and wpa_cli works. The
#   service is still called "wpa", so `service restart wpa` applies a
#   change of the switch. `wifi fallback off` switches back.
wpa -d

# Power profiles, the battery and the backlight (lp-tune). The only
# writer of the backlight; it restores the last brightness at boot and
# answers `lp-tune` and the top bar over its socket. On a board with no
# battery or backlight it applies what exists and waits.
?/bin/lp-tune lp-tune -d

# The two root helpers the desktop's apps talk to. Each asks the
# person's password (sudo group) before it does anything, keeps the
# answer for five minutes, and takes only validated verbs - never a
# shell string. lp-privd installs and removes packages (Software), and
# lp-diskd partitions, formats and checks disks (Disks). A board without
# the desktop has neither binary and skips both lines.
?/bin/lp-privd lp-privd -d
?/bin/lp-diskd lp-diskd -d

# The SSH server.
#   -F  stay in the foreground. init can only supervise a child that
#       stays alive; going to the background looks like an instant death.
#   -R  generate a host key if there is none
#   -E  log to the console
#   -r  where the host key lives. On the data partition, so it survives
#       a reboot and clients do not see the key change.
# The "?" is not decoration. An image built for a machine we have no
# dropbear binary for should come up without SSH and say so once, rather
# than have init try to exec a file that is not there every five seconds
# for the life of the board.
?/bin/dropbear dropbear -F -R -E -p 22 -r /data/dropbear_ed25519_host_key

# The clock keeper.
#   This board has no battery-backed clock. Pull the power and time
#   stops; power it up again and it starts at 1970. That makes file
#   timestamps nonsense and breaks every HTTPS certificate check.
#   -d  writes the time to /data/.clock every 30s and resyncs from the
#       network hourly. /etc/rc restores that value at boot.
# The DHCP lease has to be renewed or the router takes the address back,
# and a board that loses its address is off the network with nothing to
# notice: it has not crashed, so the watchdog is content, and memory and
# temperature are fine, so guard is content. It was the one failure with
# no automatic recovery at all - on a system whose whole purpose is
# being left alone for months.
#
# With no interface named it finds the one that answers: wlan0, then a
# USB Ethernet adapter, then the gadget port.
dhcp -d

ntp -d

# The instance's own SSH key, on EC2.
#
#   On AWS the key is not in the image. It is chosen when the instance is
#   launched and handed over through 169.254.169.254, so an image that
#   does not fetch it boots perfectly and is unreachable forever - the
#   worst failure there is, because nothing is broken and there is
#   nothing to fix from outside.
#
#   The address arrives after rc has finished (dhcp is a service too), so
#   this waits rather than deciding at boot that there is no metadata
#   service. On anything that is not EC2 it gives up in two seconds and
#   sleeps; the link-local address is answered in under a millisecond or
#   not at all.
?/bin/ec2 ec2 -d

# Scheduled jobs.
#
#   Reads /data/crontab, or /etc/crontab if there is none. With neither
#   it sleeps rather than exiting - a one-shot that exits would be
#   restarted by this supervisor every second forever, and "no jobs" is
#   the normal state for most machines.
#
#   Times are UTC. See `cron -h`.
?/bin/cron cron -d


# Watchdog.
#   The BCM2835 has a hardware countdown that reboots the board when it
#   reaches zero. The kernel driver was always in the build; nothing was
#   resetting the timer, so it was never armed and never fired.
#   Now: if the system wedges hard enough that this stops running, the
#   board reboots itself in 15 seconds instead of waiting for someone to
#   walk over and pull the power.
#   (No watchdog hardware in a VM - it exits with a clear message there.)
watchdog -t 15

# The heartbeat.
#   This board is meant to sit somewhere for months with nobody near it,
#   and there was no way to check whether it was doing that. Finding out
#   if it was alive meant logging in - and the machine you cannot log
#   into is exactly the one you wanted to know about. Silence read the
#   same as health right up until it mattered.
#   Now it says how it is, on a schedule, to a URL in /boot/beacon.conf.
#   If the reports stop, that is the signal - which is the only kind
#   that still works when the failure is "it went away".
#   With no URL set it still runs and still keeps /data/log/status.json,
#   which is the record that makes a claim about months of uptime
#   checkable afterwards rather than something to take on trust.
beacon -d

# Drives that get plugged in - and the card this system boots from being
# pulled out of a running board.
#   Without this, a USB stick or an external disk appears in `disk` and
#   nowhere else: you have to be logged in, know it came up as sdb1, and
#   know what filesystem is on it. Nobody plugs a drive into a headless
#   board and then goes to look up its device name.
#
#   -d listens on the kernel's uevent socket - the same messages udev
#   reads - so a drive is mounted within a moment of being plugged in,
#   and released when it is pulled. It mounts under /media, never
#   touches the disk this system booted from, and uses nosuid,nodev
#   because the filesystem was written by somebody else's machine.
#
#   It also runs one scan at startup, so a drive that was already
#   attached at boot is mounted too.
#
#   THE SECOND JOB IS THE IMPORTANT ONE, and it is the reason this line
#   has to be in this file and not in /etc/rc.
#
#   /data is on the SD card, /root is a bind mount off it, /boot is the
#   other partition on it, and /bin /sbin /lib /usr /opt and /srv are
#   six overlays whose upper layer is on it. Pull the card out of a
#   running board and all nine are mounts with no disk under them: the
#   kernel and the root filesystem are in RAM and perfectly fine, but
#   every command a person could type is gone, so nothing on the machine
#   can be started to put it right. It was the one failure with no way
#   back at all short of pulling the power.
#
#   This daemon is the way back, and it works only because it is already
#   running when the card goes - its own pages come from the RAM root,
#   and it takes the mounts down with the umount2 syscall rather than by
#   running /bin/umount, which is on the card that just left. After that
#   /bin is the system image again and the board is fully usable; put
#   the card back and it checks it, mounts it and rebuilds the overlays
#   by itself.
#
#   init restarts it if it dies, which is most of why it belongs here.
#   The one case that cannot be recovered from is this process dying
#   while the card is out, because init would then have to exec
#   /bin/automount off the dead card to bring it back - so it does as
#   little as possible in that state and never exits on its own.
automount -d

# Watching for the things that actually happen to a small board on the
# internet.
#   Not an antivirus - there is no signature database here and one would
#   not fit. What it looks at instead is what actually gets a board like
#   this taken: repeated SSH failures from one address, which it blocks;
#   setuid and world-writable files under /data, which is mounted
#   nosuid so a setuid file there is either pointless or somebody
#   testing something; a listening socket that was not there before,
#   which on a board that has not changed is the single most useful
#   signal there is; and a new key in authorized_keys, which is how a
#   board stays lost after it has been found.
#
#   A service rather than a thing you remember to run, because the value
#   is entirely in noticing between the times anybody looks. It sleeps
#   most of the time; the walk of /data skips the Debian tree and the
#   CPython install, which are thousands of files that legitimately look
#   like matches.
defend -d
